{"duration": 62.857, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "Sir Alexander Fleming left his petri dishes sitting out on a laboratory bench.", "start": 0.08, "end": 4.539}, {"beat": 0, "text": "Bacteria were growing in Fleming's dishes.", "start": 4.759, "end": 6.876}, {"beat": 1, "text": "When Alexander Fleming returned, one dish had a patch of mould.", "start": 7.296, "end": 10.605}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Bacteria around the mould died. Bacteria farther away stayed normal.", "start": 10.825, "end": 14.692}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Fleming said it was funny, then tested it again.", "start": 14.912, "end": 17.272}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Fleming found the mould was Penicillium.", "start": 17.492, "end": 19.588}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Fleming named the mould's weapon penicillin in 1929.", "start": 20.008, "end": 23.579}, {"beat": 2, "text": "But at a microbiology congress in London, nobody believed Fleming.", "start": 23.799, "end": 27.648}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Penicillin was too hard to isolate. It stayed mostly forgotten.", "start": 27.868, "end": 31.517}, {"beat": 2, "text": "By 1936, the world still saw no value in penicillin.", "start": 31.737, "end": 35.331}, {"beat": 3, "text": "You see, it is like one patch of mould on bread that stops everything around it from growing.", "start": 35.751, "end": 40.298}, {"beat": 4, "text": "Here is the real reason. Penicillium mould travels through the air as spores.", "start": 40.718, "end": 44.87}, {"beat": 4, "text": "In 1966, scientists traced Fleming's mould to La Touche's room, directly below his.", "start": 45.09, "end": 50.798}, {"beat": 4, "text": "So the spores drifted up from downstairs and landed on the dishes Fleming left out.", "start": 51.018, "end": 55.412}, {"beat": 5, "text": "The killer came from downstairs, not from Fleming's own bench.", "start": 55.832, "end": 58.792}, {"beat": 5, "text": "That accident gave the world its first antibiotic.", "start": 59.012, "end": 61.737}], "words": [{"w": "